 something like  the rubber ducky but I'm not sure  exactly what this thing is it seems to  be key in his plans for what he's gonna  do when he goes to Washington Township  plant that part seems clear Darlene  tries to get him to leave  he says he's not done he says he's gonna  have to go to Washington Township and  the darlene can't come and she says that  she doesn't want to like she's basically  done it's a sad moment and it kind of  felt like damn Darlene's gonna die which  is something I didn't want to have  happen but we'll get to all of that  later as they're talking Dom makes her  way into the room only to find Leon  sitting on the bed which is frightening  for her because the last time that she  saw him was at the barn at the end of  season 3 Leon doesn't remember though  he's rolling a joint watching TV and he  says have we met before she reminds him  of the bond  and it all starts to make sense to him  and we go back to that idea that now  Leon has moved on from the dark army and  he's just a hired gun  after Darlene returns the three of them  go on the road they're on the way to  Boston they're taking the scenic route  and Leon says that they're trying to  avoid all of the dark army and there are  lwas which are rich white [ __ ] he  says that they are the dark Army's  favorite clients they are lwas they stop  at a rest stop so he can go to the  bathroom which he says he needs to steal  a mirror which is a Kurt Vonnegut  reference that no one knows which leads  him to share his distaste for the fact  that nobody reads anymore  earlier Darlene had mentioned that  everything was ready to Eliot about  redistributing the money that they stole  from the day's group she thought he  should do the honors he said no this was  hers so she ends up doing that at the  rest stop with DOM it's a pretty good  moment probably my favorite part of this  episode I guess we see Tom being Dom  wondering about the specifics and if  laws were broken if crimes were  committed we see Darlene told her to  relax she gives her a kiss and we  watched the faces of everyone as they  start to get their alerts that they're  equine transfers went through that they  weren't expecting Tom looks down and she  said that everyone get this much so we  don't know exactly how much but Darlene  says it's the biggest redistribution of  wealth in the history of mankind  explains how these people wrote the laws  to benefit themselves so it didn't  matter that they were breaking the laws  and that her and Eliot basically Robin  Hood did all of them and it's pretty  cool it's enjoyable I don't know how  realistic it is but I think it's fine as  far as I'm concerned it's a TV show  I'm fine with believing that this  benefited everyone a little bit at least  in their normal lives and struck a blow  to the people who are at the top there  and what I think stands out the most  about this is that Tom moves past her  cynicism her suspicion and they kind of  just have that moment they're together  it's nice that they get that moment  considering they were just prisoners of  the dark army not all that long ago and  everything else that's happened to them  throughout this story after that they go  to the airport and we get a cameo from  Irving I wouldn't say it's a return he  just sort of pops up at the airport on  the first watch this really  me off because I couldn't decide if  there was something more going on with  this or not was it a setup with  something gonna happen it felt awkward  seeing your returner Irving is  definitely something that I wanted to  see it just felt a little bit forced I  mean it was cool to see his book it was  cool to see that he's still out there in  the world the interaction was funny just  like everything he's in but in the  background it just sort of felt like why  would she believe anything that he says  and yeah she was scared but when she  leaves it's just sort of like she leaves  the whole thing behind I don't know it  was weird to me